Abstract
In this work, we present the design and fabrication of a novel nanocomposite based on noble metal and metal oxide nanoparticles dispersed on highly porous carbon obtained via the pyrolysis of an inorganic complex and metal-organic frameworks. This nanocomposite is prepared by a two-step procedure:  first, the composite support of nanoporous carbon (NPC) is obtained by the direct carbonization of the $\mathrm{Cr}$ -benzene dicarboxylic ligand (BDC) MOF in an Argon atmosphere at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC). A mixture containing $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC and $[\mathrm{PtCl}(\mathrm{SnCl}_{3})(\mathrm{SMe}_{2})_{2}]$ is then prepared, and underflow of Argon is heated to $380^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Finally, Pt$\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles are loaded on the $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC support, and the obtained nanocomposite was denoted as $\mathrm{Pt{-}S n O_{2}/C r_{2}O_{3}{-}N P C}$ . The morphology and crystalline structure of the prepared nanocomposites were characterized using XRD, SEM, EDX, FT-IR, and XPS. In addition, the prepared nanocomposite was examined as a novel electrocatalyst for the ethanol electrooxidation reaction (EOR). The obtained results demonstrated that, compared with $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC, $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC showed higher electrocatalytic activity, lower onset potential, and a higher level of poisoning tolerance toward of ethanol oxidation in acidic media. The overall results corroborate the predominant role of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ as an excellent catalytic-enhancing agent thorough facilitating the charge transfer process and increasing the CO poisoning oxidation by the spillover of $\mathrm{OH_{ads}}$ to the $\mathrm{Pt}$ surface. Thus, the prepared $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC catalyst could be considered a promising anode catalyst for direct ethanol fuel cells.
Keywords: Nanoporous carbon; Thin oxide;  Platinum nanoparticles; Chromium (III) Oxide; Electrocatalytic oxidation; Metal-Organic Frameworks
## 1. Introduction
In the last two decades, because of the increasing global demand for energy and concerns about environmental pollution, fuel cells have been considered one of the most promising alternative energy power sources [1, 2]. As a clean technology, fuel cells generate electricity efficiently in a redox chemical reaction. Among the different types of fuel cells, direct alcohol fuel cells (DAFCs) are an excellent power sources because of their high energy efficiency, low environmental footprint, mild reaction conditions, and the simplicity of handling liquid fuel [3]. Recent research has indicated that ethanol as an alcohol  fuel is a valuable energy feedstock that offers several advantages, including nontoxicity, relatively low volatility, convenient storage, renewability, and abundant availability [4-6]. Furthermore, the complete electro-oxidation process of converting ethanol to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ provides an energy density of $8030~\mathrm{Wh}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ , which is greater than that of methanol $(6100\mathrm{Wh}\mathrm{kg}^{-1})$ and provides a clean fuel with high energy density. However, because of its slow kinetics, the electrochemical oxidation of fuels requires the use of a catalyst to achieve the desired current densities in practical applications [7]. It is worth noting that ethanol oxidation reactions (EOR) occur in a series of complex reactions, which leads to strong surface-adsorbed intermediates. These intermediates effectively accumulate on the catalyst's surface and cause the de-activation of the catalyst. Hence, searching for highly active, highly stable, and low-cost electrocatalysts for EOR is imperative for the commercialization of DAFCs [8].
Generally, platinum and platinum-based compounds are considered the most promising electrode materials with catalytic properties for the electro-oxidation of alcohols mainly because of their activity, stability, and efficiency [9-11]. However, a severe drawback of pure platinum is that it is readily poisoned by strongly adsorbed intermediates, which leads to the loss of its catalytic activity. In this regard, the incorporation of active materials, such as transition metals and metal oxides, as auxiliary agents into the structure of the active electrode material has a significant promotion effect on catalytic activity, which may offer a viable option for solving the problems encountered in using Pt catalysts [12]. This improved performance can be described based on a bi-functional mechanism that produces synergistic or electronic effects between metal catalysts and transition metal oxides. Briefly, superficial hydroxides $\mathrm{(OH_{ads})}$ ) are formed first on the surface of metal oxides and after the spillover process, they react with $\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ formed in the intermediate reaction steps to produce $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ or other insoluble products and release active sites for further electrochemical reactions [13]. A significant amount of research has been conducted to incorporate Pt-based catalysts with metal oxides, such as $\mathbf{WO}_{3}$ [14], NiO [15], $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{nO}_{2}$ [7], and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ [16], to promote ethanol electro-oxidation through a synergistic effect. The results obtained from previous reports indicated that incorporating $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ in catalyst composition has a positive impact on the electrocatalytic activity of a pure catalyst. For example, Kowal et al. reported that, compared with the Pt catalyst, the presence of $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ catalyst led to a considerable enhancement of catalyst activity in ethanol oxidation [17]. In addition, Qiu and co-workers have investigated the positive effects of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ as a co-catalyst of $\mathrm{Pt}$ catalysts in methanol electrooxidation using the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) technique [18].
Regarding the preparation of composites, the surface conditions, including surface composition, morphology, and impurity contamination, have a significant impact on the electrocatalytic performance of the prepared catalysts [19]. At present, because of the development of nanotechnology, nanoparticles (NPs) play an important role in improving the performance of catalysts. By controlling the sizes and shapes of catalysts, large surface areas and abundant catalytic active sites can be attained, which leads to the enhancement of the performance and utilization efficiency of catalysts [20]. Hence, a facile, cost-effective, and controllable synthesis method is required to produce catalyst nanoparticles without contamination. Generally, chemical synthesis is a common route used in the fabrication of bimetallic electrocatalysts. However, this method involves complicated procedures that require multiple steps. In addition, the chemical compounds, usually used in these methods are difficult to remove, and they can inhibit catalytic activity [21]. Therefore, a fast, simple method that can produce uniform nanoparticles without an additional purification step is required. In this method, bimetallic inorganic complex precursors could be used as the source of metal/metal oxide nanoparticles. After thermal treatment, this precursor lost its nonmetallic elements and served as an excellent single source for the wide dispersion of pure metal and metal oxide catalysts without chemical contamination [22]. In most previous reports, researchers utilized two different salts in varying amounts as the source of each metal. By using a complex as a single precursor with a specific ratio of metals, a precise ratio of both metals in the final synthesized catalysts could be assured.
In addition to the composition procedure and the structure of the electrocatalysts, the selection of an appropriate supporting material is important because it significantly affects the performance of the prepared electrocatalysts. The supporting material should possess a high specific surface area, good electrical conductivity, a suitable pore size, and high corrosion resistance in acidic and basic media to achieve catalysts with uniform dispersion and high utilization [23]. Among the various supports, carbon nanomaterials are suitable for this purpose because of their chemical and physical properties. Carbon nanomaterials, such as carbon nanotubes (CNTs) [24], carbon nanofibers [25], carbon nanodots [26], graphene oxide (GO) [27, 28], and graphene nanosheets [29], have been used as supporting material in fuel cells.
Nevertheless, these carbon nanomaterials require pretreatment with $\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ or ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ to ensure their surface functionalization. Because the dispersion and size of metal nanoparticles are highly dependent on the surface property, a novel carbon support that possesses all the above-mentioned physiochemical properties and that does not require pretreatment is highly desirable [30].
Over the last few years, the research in this area has been focused on the use of zeolitic imidazole frameworks (ZIFs) and/or metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) as supporting materials to improve the electrocatalytic activity of different catalysts. The MOF is an organic-inorganic hybrid crystalline porous material that is composed of single metal ions or metal clusters linked by organic ligands [31-35]. Thus, after thermal carbonization, these materials can be considered as precursors or templates for obtaining porous metal oxide/carbon composites. MOFs also exhibit several favorable properties, such as porous structures, high surface areas, and diverse structural topology. These significant features make MOFs very attractive supports for the loading of catalyst nanoparticles. For example, Shahrokhian et al. used bimetallic Ni-Co/MOF as the precursor template for the fabrication of petal-like NiCo/NiO-CoO with a rational composition embedded in a 3D ultrathin nanoporous carbon composite as a non-precious electrocatalyst in the methanol oxidation reaction [36]. Raoof et al. fabricated Cu-Pt bimetallic nanoparticles to support nanoporous carbon composite by the direct carbonization of MOF-199 as a template as well as the partial galvanic replacement of Cu metals incorporated in MOFs by $\mathrm{Pt}^{\mathrm{{IV}}}$ ions [37]. Moreover, Khan et al. demonstrated a highly efficient ORR (oxygen reduction reaction) electrocatalysts based on Pt/Ni bimetallic nanoparticles dispersed on highly porous carbon obtained via the pyrolysis of MOF-5 [30].
Based on the previous results, we propose that a new, efficient Pt-based catalyst for EOR may be created by combining a Pt catalyst, metal oxides, and a nanoporous carbon composites support. Thus, in this work, we applied a simple method to synthesize mixed $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles loaded over the $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC as a suitable support. For this purpose, we prepared $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC through the carbonization of MIL-101(Cr) as the precursor template. In the next step, we added the bimetallic complex cis- $\mathrm{\small{[PtCl(SnCl_{3})(S M e_{2})_{2}]}}$ to the $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC nanocomposite, and the sample was then heated in a furnace underflow of Ar. This process yielded pure $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles without contamination and with fine dispersion on the $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ - NPC support. The electrocatalytic activities of the as-prepared $\mathrm{Pt}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC in the electro-oxidation of ethanol in the acidic medium were investigated using the cyclic voltammetry (CV), chronoamperometry (CA), and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) techniques. The results indicate that, compared with simple $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC, the $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC showed a more effective and stable performance in the electrocatalytic oxidation of ethanol.
## 2.  Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
All reagents were of analytical grade and used without further purification. $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ , $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ , benzenedicarboxylic acid (BDC), polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP, $,\mathrm{M.W.}=360000)$ , $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ $(98\%)$ , dichloromethane, methanol, ethanol $(96\%)$ , and ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ $(98\%)$ were purchased from the Merck Company. All aqueous solutions were prepared using ultra-pure deionized water ( $18.2\mathrm{M}\Omega$ , Zolalan Sharif Company, Tehran, Iran). Also, $\mathrm{K_{2}P t C l_{4}}$ salt, dimethylsulfide $\mathrm{(SMe}_{2}\mathrm{)}$ , $\mathrm{SnCl}_{2}.2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , required for the synthesis of the complex, was purchased from Sigma-Aldrich Company.
## 2.2 Instrumentation
The electrochemical experiments were carried out by a potentiostat/galvanostat (SAMA 500-C Electrochemical analysis system, Sama, Iran) coupled with a personal computer. Glassy carbon electrodes $(\mathrm{d}=2.0\ \mathrm{mm}$ and geometric surface area of $0.0314~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ ) were used as a working electrode, $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl}$ (saturated KCl) and platinum wire were used as the reference and counter electrodes, respectively. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) data were obtained by an Autolab PGSTAT 204 with an FRA software version 4.9 (Eco Chemie, the Netherlands). EIS measurements were conducted by applying an AC voltage with amplitude of $10~\mathrm{mV}$ and the frequency changed from $40\mathrm{kHz}$ to 25 MHz. All experiments were performed at the room temperature.
The Fourier transform infrared spectra (FT-IR) were recorded on a Bomem MB-Series FTIR spectrometer using pressed KBr pellets. An X-ray diffractometer (STOE) equipped with Cu $\mathrm{K}_{\mathfrak{a}}$ irradiation $\Lambda=0.15418\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) was used for recording X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns. The surface characterizations of the synthesized catalysts were performed using field emission electron microscopy (FE-SEM, VEGA-Tescan) equipped with an energy-dispersive spectrometer (EDS). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were carried out on a spectrometer Axis Ultra DLD system (Kratos, U. K.) employing an Al $\mathrm{K}_{\mathfrak{a}}$ radiation source. The quantitative analysis of metals in catalysts was carried out by ICP-OES (VISTA-PRO, Varian, Australia).
## 2.3 Preparation of MIL-101( $C r$ -MOF) and $C r_{2}O_{3}$ -NPC nanocomposite
MIL-101(Cr-MOF) was prepared according to the previously reported procedure with some modifications [38]. In summary, a solution composed of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (4.00 g, 10.00 mmol), BDC $(1.64~\mathrm{g},10.00~\mathrm{mmol})$ and deionized water $\mathrm{40~mL}$ ) were placed in a hydrothermal chamber and heated at $218~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $18\mathrm{~h~}$ . The collected compound was washed with deionized water, methanol, dimethylformamide (DMF), and acetone. Then the obtained compound was refluxed with ethanol at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{h}$ . Finally, the pale green product filtered and dried at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 8 h under the vacuum condition. In the next step, in order to synthesize $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC nanocomposite, carbonization of MIL-101(Cr) was carried out in a tube furnace underflow of Ar. MIL-101(Cr) $\mathrm{\small{[Cr_{3}(O)X([O_{2}C)C_{6}H_{4}(C O_{2})])_{3}(H_{2}O)_{2}]}}$ (1.0 g, 1.4 mmol) was heated with a heating rate of $10~\mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}$ to $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and then hold at this temperature for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ .  After that, it cooled to ambient temperature with the cooling rate of $4~{}^{\circ}{\bf C}/{\mathrm{min}}$ . The final weight of the collected product was $0.65\ \mathrm{g}$ . The prepared nanocomposite in this step is designated as $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{NPC}$ .
## 2.4 Preparation of cis-[PtCl(SnCl3)(SMe2)2]
The cis/trans- $[\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}(\mathrm{SMe}_{2})_{2}]$ complex was prepared according to the reported procedure [39]. Specifically, dimethyl sulfide $(2.0\ \mathrm{mL},27.2\ \mathrm{mmol})$ was added to the aqueous solution of ${\mathrm{K}}_{2}{\mathrm{PtCl}}_{4}$ $(1.8~\mathrm{g},~4.3~\mathrm{mmol})$ under ${\bf N}_{2}$ gas. After 30 minutes, the water was evaporated with a heating mantle. Then a yellow mixture was extracted with dichloromethane and dried over anhydrous $\mathrm{Mg}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ . Finally, the sample was filtered, and the dichloromethane evaporated. After that, the anhydrous $\mathrm{SnCl}_{2}$ $(0.055\mathrm{~g},0.29\mathrm{~mmol})$ was added to a dichloromethane solution of cis/trans- $[\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}(\mathrm{SMe}_{2})_{2}]$ (0.113 g, 0.25 mmol) under ${\bf N}_{2}$ . After $12\mathrm{~h~}$ stirring, the $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ was removed and the product dried as an orange powder [40].
2.5 Preparation of $P t{-}S n O_{2}/C r_{2}O_{3}$ -NPC and $P t/C r_{2}O_{3}$ -NPC
For the synthesis of $\mathrm{Pt}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC, $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC (0.2 g) was added into a dichloromethane solution of cis-[PtCl(SnCl3)(SMe2)2] (0.07 g, 0.11 mmol). The mixture was stirred strongly for $1.5\mathrm{h}$ under the flow of $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ , and then the solvent was evaporated. Afterward, the resulting product was dried at room temperature. The sample was heated to $380^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a tube furnace under the flow of Ar with a heating rate of $10~{^\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}$ , and maintained at this temperature for $3\mathrm{~h~}$ , and then cooled to room temperature with a cooling rate of $2{\mathrm{~}}^{\circ}{\bf C}/{\mathrm{min}}$ . Finally, the resulted dark green powder was collected and designated as $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC. For more clarity, the sequential synthesize process involves multiple steps were shown in scheme 1. Also, for comparison, $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC was fabricated according to the following method. A mixture of 0.01 g PVP (PVP monomer: $\mathrm{Pt}=10{:}1$ molar ratio) and $0.11\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}\left(1\mathrm{~M}\right)$ were dissolved in $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol. The solution stirred at $0~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . Then, a newly prepared methanol solution of $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ $(0.1\textbf{M})$ was quickly added to the mixture. Then the $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC $(0.2~\mathrm{g})$ was rapidly added, and mixture was further stirred for 6 h. At last, the sample was washed with methanol and dried at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ . The total $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ metal loading was determined 10 wt. $\%$ in $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC and $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC by inductively coupled plasma (ICP). The Sn metal loading in $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC was also measured 7 wt. $\%$ .
2.6 Modification of the GCE with $P t{-}S n O_{2}/C r_{2}O_{3}$ -NPC and $P t/C r_{2}O_{3}$ -NPC
Prior to the GCE modification, its surface was hand-polished successively with alumina slurry in water using a polishing cloth until the electrode surface had a mirror finish. The polished electrode was placed in ethanol and sonicated for 5 minutes to remove the alumina residues from the electrode surface. After that, a homogeneous suspension was prepared by dispersing $5.0~\mathrm{mg}$ of the as-prepared $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC in $985~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ethanol and $15~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Nafion solution (15 wt. $\%$ , DuPont) under ultrasonic stirring for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . Then, a $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ drop of $\mathrm{Pt}.$ - $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC suspension was cast on the electrode surface and dried at the room temperature to form the $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC film modified GCE $(\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE). Also, a similar procedure performed for the fabrication of $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE and $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE.
