## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1 Characterization of the synthesized materials
The FT-IR spectra of the of MIL-101(Cr) (a), $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC (b), $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC (c), and Pt$\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC (d) are depicted in Fig. 1. The FT-IR spectrum of MIL-101(Cr) showed stretching vibrations of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C}}$ and $0-C=0$ located at 1612, 1527, and $1404~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . The peak observed at $756~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponded to-the-out of plane vibration of the C-H bond in the aromatic ring, which was in agreement with previous reports [41, 42]. The broadband that emerged at $3402~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was attributed to the $\mathrm{O-H}$ stretching of the adsorbed water. The peaks related to the BDC ligand at $1300{-}1650\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were eliminated after the carbonization process, which confirmed that the MIL-101(Cr) was decomposed in $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC. In general, the vibration modes of metal oxide appeared below $1000~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . Two sharp peaks displayed at 563 and $632~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , which were related to Cr-O stretching modes, identified the crystalline $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ [43]. Moreover, the Sn-O stretching vibration of tin oxide in the $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC sample was in the $500{-}650~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ region [44].
The phase structure of the synthesized samples was identified by XRD analysis. The corresponding XRD patterns of MIL-101(Cr), $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC, $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC, and $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{\cdot}$ - NPC are shown in Fig. 2. The diffraction peaks of MIL-101(Cr) were according to the standard diffraction patterns [38]. The XRD pattern of the $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC nanocomposite that produced at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ showed peaks at $2\uptheta=24.48^{\circ}$ , 33.61°, $36.24^{\circ}$ , $41.51^{\circ}$ , $50.26^{\circ}$ , $54.89^{\circ}$ , $63.55^{\circ}$ , and $65.23^{\circ}$ . These peaks corresponded to the reported $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC nanocomposite rhombohedral geometry structure (JCPDS cards file No. 00-002-1362) [45]. Since the diffraction peaks related to platinum and tin in the XRD pattern could not be detected with certainty, in this context, their presence was confirmed by EDX analysis. Compared with $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC, the XRD patterns of the $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC and $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC showed no perceptible changes during nanoparticles loading.
The surface morphology of the synthesized electrocatalysts was observed using field emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM) analysis, and the obtained micrographs are presented in Fig. 3. As shown in trace A, the MIL- $101(\mathrm{Cr})$ particles with average sizes of $200\mathrm{nm}$ had an octahedral geometry and a double-sided pyramid type shape. The surface morphology of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC resulting from the carbonization of MIL- $101(\mathrm{Cr})$ at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is shown in trace B. It had a cotton wool-like morphology and an irregular shape. The surface morphology of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}.$ - NPC was well observed in $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC (trace C) and $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC (trace D).
To determine the chemical composition of the $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC, the energy dispersive X-ray spectrometry (EDS) analysis was conducted. The peaks corresponding to C, O, Cr, Pt, and Sn elements are shown in Fig. 4A. The distribution of Pt and Sn on $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC was investigated by EDX elemental mapping. As shown in Fig. 4B, the Pt, and Sn elements showed homogeneous distribution on $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC.
The XPS investigations were carried out on the electro-catalyst $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC to confirm the presence of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and to determine the oxidation states of Pt and Sn. In the XPS survey spectrum of the $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC, the peaks of Cr 2p, O 1s, C 1s, Pt 4f, and Sn 3d were observed (Fig. 5A). As shown in Fig. 5B, the peaks located at 586.49 and 576.66 eV were related to Cr $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ and $\mathrm{Cr~}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ , respectively, and they were assigned to the $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ species [46]. In the de-convoluted Pt 4f region (Fig. 5C), the peaks that appeared at the binding energies of 71.1- $72.0\mathrm{eV}$ and 74.2-75.6 eV corresponded to Pt $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ and Pt $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ , respectively. The peaks observed at 71.56 and $74.74~\mathrm{eV}$ were attributed to $\mathrm{Pt}(0)$ .  Because the synthesis method did not include a reduction step, it was expected that a small amount of platinum would be in another oxidation state. The peaks located at 73.17 and $76.69\ \mathrm{eV}$ were assigned to $\mathrm{Pt(II)O}$ . The peaks emerged from $\mathrm{Pt(II)O}$ in addition to $\mathrm{Pt}(0)$ caused the broadness of the Pt $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ and Pt $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ peaks [47]. It should be noted that the $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ 3s peak appeared at $75.63\mathrm{eV}$ , and the interface of this peak with
Pt $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ affected the intensity ratio of $\mathrm{Pt}~4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ and $\mathrm{Pt}~4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ peaks. Moreover, the satellite of Cr 3s peak  located at $78.54\mathrm{eV}$ slightly affected the broadness of the Pt 4f peak [46].  As shown in Fig. 5D, the binding energies of the Sn 3d 5/2 and Sn 3d 3/2 peaks were found at 487.22 and 495.46 $\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively, and they were attributed to the $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ species [48].
## 3.2 Electrochemical assessments of the electrocatalytic oxidation of ethanol
Generally, cyclic voltammetry is a valuable technique that provides complementary information about the electrochemical characteristics of electrocatalysts. Therefore, to understand the electrochemical behavior of the prepared catalysts, CV studies were conducted in the supporting electrolyte in the absence of ethanol. Fig. 6A presents the CV profiles of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}.$ - NPC/GCE, $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MIL}$ (101), $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE, and $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE in $0.5{\textbf{M}}$ ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ solution at a scan rate of $50\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ . As seen in this figure, $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE did not show any obvious response. In contrast, the CVs of $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MIL}$ (101), $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE and Pt$\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE showed similar patterns. In both cases, three pairs of peaks were discernible. Peaks (I), (II), and (III) were related to the hydrogen adsorption/desorption, double layer charging, and the formation/reduction of surface oxides of Pt, respectively [24]. It should be noted that the electro-oxidation of ethanol was directly dependent on the number of available Pt sites present on the electrode surface. The electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) is an important parameter for the characterization of electrocatalysts because it shows the electrochemically active sites of the catalyst available for electrode reactions. Hence, the charge under the hydrogen desorption peak after the deduction of the double layer can be used to determine the ECSA of as-fabricated electrocatalysts. The comparison between CV curves indicated that Pt-SnO2/Cr2O3-NPC/GCE showed a larger peak area in the hydrogen adsorption/desorption regions, reflecting the larger surface area of the $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ particles. This behavior indicates that using an inorganic complex as a precursor for loading $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles provided the fine dispersion and homogeneous distribution of catalyst nanoparticles of smaller sizes onto the support. Therefore, the ECSA of $\mathrm{Pt}$ in the prepared electrocatalyst was calculated according to Eq. (1):
$$
\begin{array}{r}{E S C A=\frac{Q_{H}}{[P t]\times Q_{0}}c m^{2}}\end{array}
$$
where $\mathrm{[Pt]}$ represents the $\mathrm{Pt}$ loading $(\mathrm{mg\cm^{-2}})$ and $\mathrm{Q_{H}}$ is the charge consumed by hydrogen desorption on the $\mathrm{Pt}$ surface. $\mathrm{Q}_{0}$ was commonly taken to be $0.21~\mathrm{mC/cm}^{2}$ , which displays the charge required to oxidize a monolayer of hydrogen on a smooth polycrystalline Pt electrode [49]. The ECSAs of $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MIL}$ (101), $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE and $\mathrm{Pt}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE catalysts were 23.14, 26.05 and $32.53\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively.
After the surface characterization and evaluation of the electrochemical behavior of the fabricated electrodes, the effects of structure and composition on the electrocatalytic properties were investigated and compared for EOR. Fig. 6B shows the CV curves of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE (a), Pt-SnO2/MIL (101) (b), $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE (c), and $\mathrm{Pt}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE (d) recorded in a solution containing $0.5{\bf\delta M}$ ethanol and $0.5{\bf M}$ ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ at a scan rate of $50~\mathrm{mV~s}^{-1}$ . The obtained results indicated that $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE (a) did not show any electrocatalytic activity for the oxidation of ethanol. However, in the CV curves for $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MIL}$ (101) (b), $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE (c) and $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE (d), there were two peaks in the forward and backward scan. The anodic peak in the positive scan was ascribed to the oxidation of ethanol, and the other peak in the negative scan was derived from the oxidation of the adsorbed carbonaceous species to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The electrocatalytic performances of the prepared catalysts for EOR were compared by examining the electrochemical factors, including the onset potential $\left(\mathrm{E_{onset}}\right)$ , the current density of the forward peak $(\mathrm{j_{f}})$ , and the ratios of forward to reverse peak current densities $(\mathrm{j}_{\mathrm{f}}/\mathrm{j}_{\mathrm{r}})$ . The obtained results showed that the jf for $\mathrm{Pt}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE $(136.81\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ ) was 1.68 and 1.91 times higher than for $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MIL}$ (101) $(71.32\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\cm}^{-2})$ and $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE (81.33 $\mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}\cdot$ ), which demonstrated the enlarged catalytic activity for EOR. Moreover, the maximum catalytic currents were normalized in the corresponding ECSA and the loading amounts of the metals. As shown in Fig. 6C, the specific activity (SA) and mass catalytic activity of Pt$\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE were $2.82\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and $853.3\mathrm{\mA\mg^{-1}}$ , which were larger than the corresponding amounts of $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MIL}$ (101) $\mathrm{1.86~mA~cm^{-2}}$ , $484.89\mathrm{\mA\mg^{-1}},$ ) and $\mathrm{Pt/Cr_{2}O_{3}}.$ -
NPC/GCE $(2.09\mathrm{\mAcm^{-2}}$ and $542.2\mathrm{\mA\mg^{-1}},$ ). Furthermore, the ratios of the forward peak current density to the reverse one $(\mathrm{j}_{\mathrm{f}}/\mathrm{j}_{\mathrm{r}})$ confirmed the toleration of these catalysts to the adsorbed carbonaceous  species [50]. The ratio of $\mathrm{j}_{\mathrm{f}}/\mathrm{j}_{\mathrm{r}}$ was obtained as 1.41, 1.30 and 1.04 for Pt$\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE, $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MIL}$ (101) and $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE, respectively. This result indicates that the presence of $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ enhanced the effective oxidation of ethanol in the final product and facilitated the removal of the poisoning species from the surface of the catalyst. Moreover, the close examination of the initial part of the forward anodic peaks (inset of Fig. 6B) showed that the peak current density of the ethanol oxidation was $10~\mathrm{\mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ on Pt$\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE with an $\mathrm{E_{onset}}$ of $0.17\mathrm{~V~}$ , which was obviously lower than that of Pt$\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MIL}$ (101) (0.38 V) and $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE (0.24 V). The negative shifting of the onset potential revealed that $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE effectively improved the reaction kinetics and decreased the overpotential of the electro-oxidation of ethanol. The results obtained from the CVs are listed in Table 1, which demonstrates the superior electrocatalytic activity of Pt$\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE.
Regarding the analysis and the experimental results, three major factors may be considered in explaining the enhanced electrocatalytic activity on the surface of $\mathrm{Pt}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE: 1) First, the structure and character of the supports had noticeable effects on the electrocatalytic activity of the catalyst, which was due to the direct interaction of the support catalyst. This result indicates that MOF-derived porous carbon supports with high porosity and huge surface areas could enhance the electron transfer kinetics and the accessible surface area of catalysts [27]. 2) Moreover, the fine dispersion, efficient adhesion, and prevention of catalyst agglomeration in the support had significant effects on electrocatalytic activity. These results indicate the efficacious use of an inorganic complex in-situ preparation and the fine dispersion of mixed metals and metal oxides in a porous carbon support. Moreover, the homogeneous dispersion was shown to increase the accessible surface area and the number of effective sites of catalyst nanoparticles in an electrocatalytic reaction.
3) It is perspicuously accepted that the metal/metal oxide binary composition of catalysts through co-action and synergistic effects increases catalytic activity. These synergistic effects can be divided into two major categories: bi-functional and ligand. In a bi-functional mechanism, the oxophilic nature of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ facilitates the formation of $\mathrm{OH_{ads}}$ and spillover to the Pt surface. Therefore, $\mathrm{OH_{ads}}$ reacted with the adsorbed carbon monoxide $\mathrm{{CO}_{\mathrm{{ads}}})}$ ) to produce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , and this action refined the $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ active sites, enabling them to continue the electrochemical reaction [51]. Generally, the reactions in the ethanol oxidation on the surface of $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{\cdot}$ 一 NPC/GCE were expressed by the following reactions (Eqs. 2-4):
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathrm{Pt}+\mathrm{CH_{3}C H_{2}O H}_{\mathrm{ads}}\to\dots\to\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}}+\mathrm{H}^{+}+\mathrm{e}^{-}}\\ &{\mathrm{SnO_{2}}/\mathrm{Cr_{2}O_{3}}+\mathrm{H_{2}O}\to(\mathrm{SnO_{2}}/\mathrm{Cr_{2}O_{3}}){\mathrm{-OH}}_{\mathrm{ads}}+\mathrm{H}^{+}+\mathrm{e}^{-}}\\ &{\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}}+(\mathrm{SnO_{2}}/\mathrm{Cr_{2}O_{3}}){\mathrm{-OH}}_{\mathrm{ads}}\to\mathrm{Pt}+\mathrm{SnO}_{2}+\mathrm{Cr_{2}O_{3}}+\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\mathrm{H}^{+}+\mathrm{e}^{-}}\end{array}
$$
$$
\mathrm{Pt}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{OH}_{\mathrm{ads}}+\mathrm{Pt}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}\longrightarrow\mathrm{2Pt}+\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\mathrm{H}^{+}+\mathrm{e}^{-}
$$
In contrast, the ligand effects influenced the chemisorption energy of the adsorbed intermediates on the surface of the catalyst. In addition, the electronic structure modification, which was due to the partial electron transfer between $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ and $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ , increased the electrocatalytic capability.
Table 1 shows the results of the comparison between $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE and the results of previous works based on ESCA, $\mathrm{E_{onset}}$ , forward peak potential $\mathrm{(E_{p})}$ , mass activity, and jf/jb. These data indicate that the proposed catalyst showed acceptable electrocatalytic performance compared with the results of pervious research.
## 3.3 Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) studies
The sensitive electrochemical technique of EIS was used to provide additional information about the ethanol oxidation mechanism. For this purpose, EIS experiments were conducted at different potentials. Fig. 7A-D presents the Nyquist plots of $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE and Pt$\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE in a solution containing $0.5\textbf{M}$ ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ and $0.5\textbf{M}$ ethanol in a bias potential ranging from 0.1 to $0.8\mathrm{V}$ . The results indicated that by changing the bias potential, EIS showed different Nyquist patterns, which means that the mechanism of the reactions was strongly dependent on the applied potential.
Fig. 7A shows $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE in the potential range of $0.1\mathrm{-}0.3\mathrm{~V~}$ in a straight line and a large arc, respectively, in the first quadrant. Based on the Nyquist plots, it is suggested that the slow kinetics was probably due to the adsorption of EOR intermediates, especially $\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ after the ethanol dehydrogenation, which were strongly adsorbed on Pt sites and prevented the adsorption and dehydrogenation of ethanol molecules. Therefore, the ethanol dehydrogenation reaction was the rate-determining step (reaction 2) [60]. When the potential reached $-0.4\mathrm{~V~}$ , the impedance profiles showed a clockwise loop in the first and fourth quadrants. This behavior revealed that the intermediates were oxidized gradually and that $\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ coverage was decreased. Thus the Pt sites were again accessible for the absorption and oxidation of the ethanol molecules. Hence, the rate-determining step in the ethanol electro-oxidation in a transitional situation changed from the oxidation of $\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ to the adsorption and dehydrogenation of ethanol and vice versa [21]. It should be noted that according to a previous report, this potential was lower than the potential for the onset of $\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ oxidation, and the intermediate oxidation reaction could not be explained by $\mathrm{OH_{ads}}$ incorporation via a bi-functional mechanism [61]. Therefore, in this phenomenon (i.e. $\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ oxidation at lower potential) the promotional effect of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ on the Pt catalyst could be explained by the ligand effect. According to this hypothesis, the electronic interaction between $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ is helpful in weakening the adsorption strength of $\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ on the Pt surface. This effect facilitated $\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ oxidation and reduces the accumulation of carbonaceous intermediates on the surface of the catalyst [8]. At a potential of $0.5\mathrm{~V~}$ , the impedance curves suddenly appeared in the second and third quadrants with negative values of real impedance. This phenomenon may be attributed to the oxidation of $\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ intermediates due to the formation of chemisorbed hydroxyl species, which indicates that $\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ oxidation is very slow, and it may be considered the rate-determining step (reactions 4 and 5). Finally, by increasing the potential to the range of $0.7\mathrm{-}0.8\mathrm{~V~}$ , the impedance arcs returned to the first quadrant in positive values. In the mentioned potential, the $\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ intermediates were completely removed from the Pt surfaces, and the active catalyst sites recovered, thus continuing the reaction. At such a high overpotential, the hydroxyl groups, i.e., $\mathrm{OH_{ads}}$ , on the surface of $\mathrm{Pt}$ catalysts could be oxidized to form platinum oxide (PtO). The resulting PtO species covered the $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ surface and prevented a further ethanol oxidation reaction. This result was inconsistent with the results of the CVs shown in Fig. 6B at the potential range of 0.7-0.8, in which the current densities declined significantly. In this potential, the oxidation of $\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ with surface oxygen atoms was the rate-determining step (reaction 6) [62].
$$
\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}+\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{O}\longrightarrow2\mathrm{Pt}+\mathrm{CO}_{2}
$$
According to the results obtained in this study, $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE (A-B) and $\mathrm{Pt/Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}.$ - NPC/GCE (C-D) nearly showed similar impedance profiles with some differences, which are explained as follows:
(1) The comparison between the impedance profiles indicated that the diameter of semi-circles considerably decreased on $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE. This result showed smaller charge transfer resistance $(\mathrm{R_{ct}})$ and faster electron transfer kinetics on $\mathrm{Pt}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE, which were due to larger surface area and more active sites in the oxidation reaction of ethanol.
(2) At the potential of $0.4{\mathrm{~V}},$ , the impedance profile of $\mathrm{Pt}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE showed a clockwise loop, while it showed a semicircle in $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE. Based on the observed Nyquist plots, it could be concluded that because of the considerable increase in of the surfaceactive sites of $\mathrm{Pt}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE, the ethanol adsorption and dehydrogenation reaction rate increased significantly.
(3) In addition, the onset potential of impedance with a negative value on $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{\cdot}$ NPC/GCE (0.50 V) was more negative than that on $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE (0.60 V). This result implies that the presence of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ species, because of its oxophilic nature, facilitates the formation of $\mathrm{OH_{ads}}$ and spillover to the $\mathrm{Pt}$ surface. These hydroxyl groups successfully reacted with $\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ on the $\mathrm{Pt}$ surface to form $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ at lower potentials in a bi-functional mechanism.
## 3.4 Long-term stability
In addition to the high electro-activity of a catalyst, stability is an important factor in the evaluation of its efficiency. Generally, a chronoamperometry method with a large time window is applied in the study of the activity and stability of electrocatalysts. Hence, in this study, the stability of $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE and $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE was examined using the 2000 s chronoamperometric test in a $0.5{\mathrm{~M~}}$ ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ solution containing $0.5{\mathrm{~M~}}$ ethanol at a constant potential corresponding to the forward peak potentials in the related CVs (Fig. 8A). As this figure shows, each electrode exhibited a current decay during the initial period because of the formation and accumulation of intermediate species during the EOR [63]. The inset in Fig. 8A shows that $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE demonstrated the lowest current decay after 300 s. Furthermore, after reaching the current density in a relatively stable situation, $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{\cdot}$ NPC/GCE exhibited higher current densities over the entire period. Moreover, long-term stability was confirmed by measuring the current density response in ethanol oxidation in 300 successive cycles in a solution containing $0.5{\bf\delta M}$ ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ and $0.5{\mathrm{~M~}}$ ethanol. Fig. 8B shows the plot of the anodic peak current densities in the progressive scan as a function of the sweep number. It was observed that in $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE, the anodic peak current density increased during the initial cycles (until 43rd sweep). This behavior indicated that the presence of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticle and the Pt catalyst caused the effective removal of the adsorbed intermediates from the catalyst surface and facilitated the access of the ethanol molecules to the active sites. Subsequently, the anodic peak current density decreased gradually with potential cycling, which was due to the accumulation of poisonous species and changes the structure of the catalyst. After the completion of the cycles, it was observed that in $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE and $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE, the forward current density remained at $68\%$ and $47\%$ of the initial value, respectively. According to these results, the $\mathrm{Pt}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{NPC}/\mathrm{GCl}$ E exhibited good stability in EOR.
## 3.5 Effects of ethanol concentration, potential sweep rates, and switching potentials
To evaluate the capacity of the prepared catalyst against EOR, the effects of the different ethanol concentrations on the jf were investigated. Fig. 9A discloses the effect of ethanol concentration on the $\mathrm{Pt}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE in 0.5 M ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ solution, in which the height of jf increased as the concentration of ethanol was increased. The plot of jf versus the ethanol concentration (inset of Fig. 9A) showed a linear segment up to $0.7{\bf M}$ , which then decreased to a steady state. This behavior may have been due to the contamination of Pt active sites by the poisonous intermediates. To study the EOR mechanism, the effects of various potential scan rates were examined in the electrocatalytic behavior of $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE. Fig. 9B presents the CVs of the ethanol oxidation on $\mathrm{Pt}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE at different scan rates from 0.005 to $0.8\mathrm{~V~s~}^{-1}$ and the inset shows the dependency of $\mathbf{j}_{\mathrm{f}}$ versus the square root of the potential scan rate $(\mathfrak{v}^{1/2})$ . The linear relationship between $\mathbf{j}_{\mathrm{f}}$ and $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathfrak{v}^{1/2}$ indicated that EOR was a diffusion-controlled process [64]. Furthermore, to understand the relation between Pt oxide species and ethanol oxidation, the effects of switching at the $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE were investigated (Fig. 9C). The results showed that by increasing the switching potential, the forward peak potential and current density remained relatively unchanged, whereas the peak current density in the reverse scan decreased and the reverse peak potential shifted to a negative direction. A plausible explanation for this phenomenon is that by extending the potential window to the positive direction in the forward scan, the formation of Pt oxide was accelerated, and the poisoning of the catalyst’s surface was increased. Therefore, this process showed decreasing backward current density and potential shifting in a negative direction. Thus, the oxidation process through intermediate was facilitated by preventing PtO formation and keeping the catalyst surface clean [29].
## 4. Conclusion
In this work, the $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{NPC}$ composite was synthesized through the direct carbonization of Cr-MOF as a template without the addition of carbon sources. The synthesized $\mathrm{PtCl}(\mathrm{SnCl}_{3})(\mathrm{SMe}_{2})_{2}$ complex was then loaded on the support and after thermal treatment, the $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles were homogeneously distributed on/into the support (i.e., $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{\cdot}$ - NPC/GCE). After surface characterization using different methods, the results confirmed the formation of $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles. After evaluating the electrochemical performance, the CVs of the $\mathrm{Pt}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE indicated an improvement in the electrocatalytic activity of the EOR with respect to various electrochemical parameters, including ESCA, jf, jf/jb, Eonset, and $\mathrm{E_{p}}$ . The enhanced performance of $\mathrm{Pt}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -NPC/GCE could be attributed to the synergetic effects of the different components. Moreover, suitable supports with high surface area increased the uniform distribution and decreased the accumulation of the catalyst nanoparticles. Catalysts with larger surface areas and accessible active sites increased the adsorption and dehydrogenation of ethanol molecules during the oxidation reaction. Moreover, the high electrical conductivity of the nanoporous carbon support increased electrons transport during the electro-oxidation reaction.  The $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanoparticles enhanced the kinetics of $\mathrm{OH_{ads}}$ formation and led to the effective elimination of the poisoning intermediate, thus increasing the efficiency and durability of the prepared catalyst.
